Acute Viral Laryngitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Hoarseness or aphonia, Dry throat and cough, Recent URI features

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-003
Reason for Visit: Complete loss of voice and severe paroxysmal coughing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting rapid, complete voice loss over 6 hours combined with severe, gagging coughing fits and throat pain. Given the acute onset of aphonia and respiratory irritation, an immediate on-site clinical assessment is required to visualize the oropharynx, assess airway patency, and definitively rule out life-threatening acute epiglottitis or bacterial tracheitis. Delaying this assessment until standard operating hours poses an unacceptable risk of missing an impending airway obstruction, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I completely lost my voice this afternoon, and this tickle in my throat makes me cough until I retch.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 72 hours ago with a mild rhinorrhea and generalized fatigue. Over the last 12 hours, the patient developed a profoundly dry, scratchy throat and an irritating tickling sensation in the larynx. This rapidly progressed to a complete loss of voice (aphonia) and a dry, non-productive cough. Mild odynophagia is present, primarily due to the mechanical trauma of frequent, forceful throat clearing. Denies dyspnea, drooling, or high fevers.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with family. No immediate occupational vocal demands for the next 48 hours. Low fall risk.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Speaks only in a forced, painful whisper. Frequent dry coughing observed during the interview. No audible stridor or tripod positioning.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals mild erythema of the posterior pharyngeal wall. No tonsillar exudate or enlargement. Uvula is midline. Neck is supple without significant cervical lymphadenopathy. Respiratory examination demonstrates clear lung fields bilaterally with no wheeze or crepitations. Indirect visualization (if equipped) or clinical inference suggests erythematous and edematous vocal cords without structural lesions.
Environmental Observations: Adequate indoor ventilation. Humidifier noted in the bedroom.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Self-medicating with over-the-counter throat lozenges without symptomatic relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Acute Viral Laryngitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Acute Epiglottitis - Ruled out due to the absence of drooling, absence of stridor, normal respiratory rate, and absence of severe systemic toxicity or tripod positioning.
Bacterial Tracheitis - Unlikely given the lack of high-grade fever, absence of purulent sputum, and clear bilateral air entry without lower respiratory signs.
Vocal Cord Nodule/Polyp - Ruled out as the primary acute cause due to the concurrent upper respiratory infectious symptoms (rhinorrhea, low-grade fever) and the acute timeline of onset.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The sudden onset of aphonia and severe paroxysmal coughing required urgent on-site evaluation to ensure airway integrity and exclude rapidly progressive infectious emergencies such as epiglottitis. Clinical examination confirmed a stable, patent airway and localized upper respiratory viral signs consistent with acute viral laryngitis. While benign in its natural course, the severity of the cough and the vocal distress necessitated immediate evaluation, reassurance, and symptomatic management planning that could not safely or reasonably be delayed until the next in-hours period, fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Advised strict vocal rest. Recommended regular Paracetamol 1000mg up to four times daily for mild odynophagia. Flexible laryngoscopy referral reserved for cases failing to resolve after 14 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote rapid healing of the vocal cords, maintain absolute voice rest rather than whispering; whispering places more mechanical strain on the vocal cords than normal speech. Keep a notepad and pen in hand at all times as a physical reminder to write rather than attempt to vocalize.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine review with regular General Practitioner in 7-10 days if hoarseness persists.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if experiencing difficulty breathing, noisy breathing (stridor), inability to swallow saliva, or a sudden, severe worsening of throat pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive and based on clinical presentation; underlying structural lesions require specialist visualization if symptoms persist.
Clinical Course: The acute viral phase typically resolves within 7-10 days. Vocal cord inflammation may linger, causing mild hoarseness for up to two weeks post-infection.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate emergency medical care if you develop high fevers, start drooling, or notice any high-pitched whistling sounds when you breathe in.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The vocal cords are currently swollen and inflamed from a viral infection. Antibiotics are completely ineffective. The only definitive treatment to restore your voice is absolute rest to allow the localized swelling to subside.
Actionable Steps: Hydrate frequently with room-temperature water. Utilize a cool-mist humidifier in the bedroom to prevent mucosal drying during sleep. Avoid all smoke exposure.

SMS INFORMATION
